From: Jean-Michael Celerier <jmcelerier@sat.qc.ca>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] rhi: vk: include every declared shader stage in resource barriers

QRhiPassResourceTracker collapsed a shader resource binding's stage flags
to a single stage ("pick the earlier stage (as this is going to be
dstAccessMask)"), so a binding declared VertexStage | FragmentStage
produced a barrier with dstStageMask = VK_PIPELINE_STAGE_VERTEX_SHADER_BIT
alone.

That is not sufficient. Per the Vulkan spec, "Synchronization and Cache
Control" / "Pipeline Stages":

    If a synchronization command includes a destination stage mask, its
    second synchronization scope only includes execution of the pipeline
    stages specified in that mask and any logically later stages. Its
    second access scope only includes memory accesses performed by
    pipeline stages explicitly specified in the destination stage mask.

    NOTE: access scopes do not interact with the logically earlier or
    later stages for either scope - only the stages the application
    specifies are considered part of each access scope.

The logically-later expansion therefore buys ordering, not visibility: a
write made available by the barrier is never made visible to a fragment
shader read, because FRAGMENT_SHADER is not in the second access scope.
Synchronization validation reports this as

    SYNC-HAZARD-READ-AFTER-WRITE ... vkCmdDraw(): Hazard READ_AFTER_WRITE
    for VkImageView ..., usage: SYNC_FRAGMENT_SHADER_SHADER_SAMPLED_READ,
    prior_usage: SYNC_IMAGE_LAYOUT_TRANSITION, write_barriers:
    SYNC_VERTEX_SHADER_SHADER_SAMPLED_READ|...

for every draw that samples a render-target texture through a binding
visible to both the vertex and the fragment stage - the common case for
any renderer that declares one visibility mask for all of its samplers.

The collapse is also unsound in a way no ordering argument covers: stage
ordering is defined per pipeline type, so for a VertexStage | ComputeStage
binding COMPUTE_SHADER is neither explicitly specified nor logically later
than VERTEX_SHADER, and drops out of both scopes.

Turn BufferStage/TextureStage into flag types and carry the full set
through the tracker, so dstStageMask/dstAccessMask name every stage the
binding declares. Stages are unioned rather than minimised when a resource
is registered twice in a pass; a superset of the second access scope is
always correct.

Measured on the ossia/score Vulkan render engine (241-test gfx suite,
NVIDIA 610.57.04, VK_LAYER_KHRONOS_validation with synchronization
validation enabled): SYNC-HAZARD-READ-AFTER-WRITE 707 -> 0, with no
change to any other validation message and no test-result change.
